  "title": "Feedback cancels in balanced loops",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "In a loop of four rule-60 rings of s cells with contact c_i at cell k_i of R_i and cell 0 of R_(i+1), k_i in {2, s - 2}, each contact reading its left neighbours (law 12, 12) or both neighbours (6, 6) on both rings: (1) over GF(2), C_i = S_i + z a^(f_i) C_(i-1) + z a^(b_(i+1)) C_(i+1) with a = z/(1+z), and after substitution the C_i term always cancels and the C_(i+2) term cancels exactly when k_0 + k_1 + k_2 + k_3 = 2s, giving C_i = S_i + z a^(f_i) S_(i-1) + z a^(b_(i+1)) S_(i+1); (2) in a balanced loop where no law-(6,6) contact c_j has k_j = s - 2 or k_(j+1) = 2, every contact binds both its rings at every lag at every size, through the ends of the chains it reads (from s = 4 with law (12,12) everywhere); (3) with law (12,12), c_i needs R_(i-1) and R_(i+2) exactly at the lags where C(T - 2, m) is odd for some m in [f_i, f_i + f_(i-1) - 1], respectively [b_(i+1), b_(i+1) + b_(i+2) - 1].",
